## 7. What Are Generics in Java?

Generics allow us to write reusable classes and methods that work with different reference types while providing compile-time type checking.

Imagine that we need separate storage classes for:

- Integers
- Strings
- Doubles

Without generics, we might create three different classes:

```java
class IntegerBox {
    Integer value;
}

class StringBox {
    String value;
}

class DoubleBox {
    Double value;
}
```

This repeats the same basic logic.

With generics, we can create one reusable class:

```java
class Box<T> {
    T value;
}
```

Here, `T` is a type parameter. It acts as a placeholder for a type.

We can then write:

```java
Box<Integer> integerBox = new Box<>();
Box<String> stringBox = new Box<>();
Box<Double> doubleBox = new Box<>();
```

The same class can be used with different types.

Common type parameter names include:

- `T`: Type
- `E`: Element
- `K`: Key
- `V`: Value

These are naming conventions, not special Java keywords.

The real benefit is that the compiler can enforce the intended type.

```java
ArrayList<String> names = new ArrayList<>();

names.add("Shivam");
names.add("Kunal");

// names.add(100); // Compile-time error
```

The list is intended to hold strings. Java therefore rejects an attempt to add an integer directly.

Generics do not magically prevent every possible type error, but they make ordinary collection usage much safer.

