## 8. Building a Generic Version of Our Custom Array List

Now we can build a generic version of our custom array list.

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class CustomGenArrayList<T> {

    private Object[] data;
    private static final int DEFAULT_SIZE = 10;
    private int size = 0;

    public CustomGenArrayList() {
        data = new Object[DEFAULT_SIZE];
    }

    public void add(T value) {
        if (size == data.length) {
            resize();
        }

        data[size++] = value;
    }

    private void resize() {
        Object[] temp = new Object[data.length * 2];

        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            temp[i] = data[i];
        }

        data = temp;
    }

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public T get(int index) {
        if (index < 0 || index >= size) {
            throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException();
        }

        return (T) data[index];
    }

    public int size() {
        return size;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return Arrays.toString(
                Arrays.copyOf(data, size)
        );
    }
}
```

Let's examine the key differences.

### Difference 1: The class declares a type parameter

```java
public class CustomGenArrayList<T>
```

The type parameter `T` allows a caller to specify the type of elements the list is intended to handle.

For example:

```java
CustomGenArrayList<Integer> numbers =
        new CustomGenArrayList<>();

CustomGenArrayList<String> names =
        new CustomGenArrayList<>();
```

### Difference 2: The internal array uses `Object[]`

```java
private Object[] data;
```

Java does not permit directly creating an array of a type parameter:

```java
data = new T[10]; // Compile-time error
```

Why? The central reason is type erasure, which we will examine next.

### Difference 3: The `add()` method uses `T`

```java
public void add(T value) {
    if (size == data.length) {
        resize();
    }

    data[size++] = value;
}
```

For the intended use of this list:

```java
CustomGenArrayList<Integer> numbers =
        new CustomGenArrayList<>();
```

The compiler checks the `add()` operation as accepting `Integer` values.

This works:

```java
numbers.add(10);
numbers.add(20);
```

This fails at compile time:

```java
// numbers.add("Hello");
```

The integer literals are autoboxed to `Integer` objects when passed to the method.

### Difference 4: The `get()` method returns `T`

```java
public T get(int index) {
    return (T) data[index];
}
```

The array provides an element through an `Object` reference. The cast expresses that the method returns the element as type `T`.

The cast is unchecked because Java cannot generally verify the original generic type parameter at runtime. Correct generic usage makes this cast safe, but misuse of the class can still cause runtime errors.

