## 14. Comparing `int[]`, `Integer[]`, and `Object[]`

These three array types are easy to confuse.

| Feature | `int[]` | `Integer[]` | `Object[]` |
|---|---|---|---|
| Stores | Primitive `int` values | References to `Integer` objects | References to objects |
| Default element value | `0` | `null` | `null` |
| Accepts `String` objects directly | No | No | Yes |
| Accepts `Integer` objects | Not as objects; unboxing is needed for an `int` assignment | Yes | Yes |
| Can be created directly | `new int[10]` | `new Integer[10]` | `new Object[10]` |

For example:

```java
int[] a = new int[3];
Integer[] b = new Integer[3];
Object[] c = new Object[3];
```

All three arrays are valid, but they store different kinds of elements.

The first stores primitive integers. The second stores references to integer wrapper objects. The third stores references to objects of different possible types.

Even when an `Integer` object is stored in an `Object[]`, the object remains an `Integer`. The array's declared component type does not change the actual class of the object.

